  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nThe low levels remain saturated to near saturated tonight into \nSunday morning which will keep the potential for fog in place, as \nwell as patchy drizzle. Dense fog will again be fairly sparse, but \nsome locations will see the visibility fall to a half mile or less \nat times.\n\nOverall, Sunday looks to be a pretty dreary day with low level \nsaturation deepening through the day. While the visibilities will \nlikely improve a bit through the morning, stratus will remain fairly \nlow and will not dissipate. As this low level moisture deepens, a \nweak wave will approach from the south and bring slightly cooler \ntemperatures aloft and a bit more elevated moisture. This should \nallow for scattered showers to develop, but with only very weak \ninstability thunder looks unlikely. As this wave rotates northward, \na slightly stronger wave will move eastward through northern NE into \nnorthwest IA which should keep an expanding area of rain in place \nacross the area. Given the placement of the upper level wave and the \nassociated instability, parts of south central and southeast SD down \nthe Missouri River will likely see the better chances for an inch or \nso of rain. The 12z Nam, RRFS and HRRR all paint these higher \nrainfall amounts near and south of I-90.\n\nThis wave slowly moves through on Monday, so expect to see showers \nslowly dissipate from west to east through late afternoon. Stratus \nwill once again be difficult to shake as the push behind this wave \nis pretty weak and lighter northeast to east flow will be in place in \nthe lower levels. Some hints in the soundings that clearing will be \npossible on Monday night, but with light winds and a moist boundary \nlayer, fog will then become moire likely for late Monday night into \nTuesday morning.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday will see some weak upper level ridging with \nafternoon temperatures likely only rising into the 60s. \n\nWednesday night into Thursday a trough of low pressure will approach \nfrom the west and bring a chance for showers and isolated \nthunderstorms. Severe weather looks unlikely. For now the better \nchances will likely be west of I-29.\n\nTemperatures should remain a touch below normal Thursday into Friday \nwith some potential for spotty showers. Highs generally in the upper \n60s to lower 70s.",
